#title Amusewiki
#author Marco Pessotto
#DELETED WIP

Amusewiki is now 12 years old and I consider it quite mature. It's
starting to show its age though.

** Considerations

*** Things which were done right

 - The markup is not famous as Markdown (the clear winner in the
   lightweight markups race, chatbots speak that...) but I still think
   it's sensible and nice to use.

 - Muse repository layout. Probably it should have had a subdirectory
   with actual texts, and another for some meta things, but it's not
   terrible either.

 - The Text::Amuse parser proved fine.

 - Perl. Often deemed as dead, old, etc. it's everywhere and it's
   stable and rock-solid. No regrets here.

 - Debian packaging. That's the only way to keep up with security
   updates. We still have too many packaged modules and fonts which
   need to be maintained. The main culprit is Net::ACME2. Too many
   modules for that, just to get a Let's Encrypt certificate.

 - Xapian. Fast, reliable, got no problem really.

*** Things which are not going to last

 - Bootstrap frontend. At the time it was a no-brainer, now it's a
   problem. Things like the grid system are terribly obsoleted by the
   super nice CSS grid, which is built into any modern browser.
   Upgrading to newer versions is *very* unrewarding.

 - Bootstrap themes. A problem stacked on another... Too many options
   are not good, especially if the manpower is limited.

 - Jquery. Similar problem as bootstrap. No-brainer at the time, now
   mostly useless. Feels like another age.

*** DBIx::Class, aka DBIC

It works, but it's not seeing any update, and most likely it's not
going to have any. At the time it was the "jewel of the crown" of
CPAN. It's not going away any time soon either, but the problem is
that I'm not in love with ORMs any more. Part of the problem is that
they want to be DB-agnostic, so it should work the same on SQLite,
MySQL/MariaDB, PostgreSQL. So basically you're cutting yourself out of
any DB-specific features (and I'm thinking about PostgreSQL,
specifically), while having to cope with random problems because you
want to support too many back-ends.

Honestly I like to have pieces of clean SQL in the code, as it's very
clear what is doing.

*** Catalyst

I loved it at the time, now I'd go Mojolicious (which is also packaged
by Debian) like everybody else...

*** LaTeX

=/opt/amusewiki-texlive/2026= is 800Mb, despite the efforts to package
a stripped down version. However, I have yet to find another solution,
considering that we also want to do complex things. So for the moment
we have to live with that. While I used to follow what was happening
in the TeX land, I admit currently I'm totally in the dark though.



 - camps + db
